From: the SK-D840N 7.3 line
Subject: [PATCH] net: zx279133: do not delegate the transmit checksum to a
 half-working data path

The chip's transmit path can add up the pseudo header, but nothing writes
the finished TCP checksum into the header of the frame that leaves for a
client.  The driver asks for NETIF_F_HW_CSUM on that basis, so the stack
hands the checksum over instead of computing it, and the frames go out with
a placeholder in the TCP header.

Measured on the board - 7.3.4, the 2.5G jack, the client on the wire: every
TCP frame the board sent carried a checksum that does not verify.  The same
value appears in every retransmission, while the timestamp option inside
those same frames changes every time, which is what a placeholder looks
like.  The peer dropped those frames without a word: its SYN-ACK came back
over the wire and the connection never completed, and ICMP over the very
same path was perfect in both directions.  The board's own counters agree -
tx_hw_csum_packets was 127 and tx_sw_csum_packets was 0, so every frame
took the hardware path.

So the flag has to go.  With it off the stack computes the checksum in
software, the frames that leave the chip carry a correct one, and TCP works
on the jack.  The price is the checksum arithmetic in the CPU, which is
what the hardware was supposed to save.

The same half-working checksum is declared in the 6.18 generation, so its
TCP was broken in the same way; what the 6.18 line never did was measure
TCP rather than ICMP.

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
@@ -80,7 +80,29 @@
 #define zx279133_tx_selector		0x0f
 #define zx279133_tx_pon_control		0x08000000
 #define zx279133_tx_word4_bit23		0
-#define zx279133_tx_hw_csum		1
+/*
+ * The transmit checksum is only half there.
+ *
+ * This SoC's own data path can add up the pseudo header, but nothing writes
+ * the finished TCP checksum into the header of the frame that leaves for a
+ * client.  Asking for NETIF_F_HW_CSUM on that basis makes the stack hand
+ * the checksum over instead of computing it, and the frame goes out with a
+ * placeholder in the TCP header.
+ *
+ * Measured on the board: every TCP frame the board sent carried a checksum
+ * that does not verify - the same value in every retransmission, while the
+ * timestamp option inside those same frames changed each time, which is
+ * what a placeholder looks like.  The peer dropped those frames without a
+ * word: its SYN-ACK came back over the wire and the connection never
+ * completed, while ICMP over the very same path was perfect.  The board's
+ * own counters agreed - tx_hw_csum_packets 127, tx_sw_csum_packets 0.
+ *
+ * So the flag goes: with it off the stack computes the checksum in
+ * software, the frames carry a correct one, and TCP works on the jack.  The
+ * price is the checksum arithmetic in the CPU, which is what the hardware
+ * was supposed to save.
+ */
+#define zx279133_tx_hw_csum		0
 /*
  * Bit 24 of the IDM control word.  The vendor's own idm_init() sets it
  * ((v & 0xf00fffff) | 0x01f00000), and the proven vendor image reads
